# Peka — Story & World

> Companion to `game-design.md`. The story is told **wordlessly** — through
> environment, animation, and image only. No dialogue, no text. This is the
> design bible; players *infer* all of it.

---

## Logline

A being that went down into the dark was torn in two at the bottom of the world.
To become whole, its two halves must climb back to the light **together** — for
apart, in the dark, a half becomes a shadow.

---

## Premise — the Split

Something small went down into the cave, alone. At its deepest point lies the
place where the world's pull turns over — where **down becomes up**. There, the
descender was split in two: one half kept by the pull that *sinks*, one half
claimed by the pull that *rises*.

Neither half is whole. Each can only fall its own way now — one toward the floor,
one toward the ceiling of every passage. That is the gravity-flip: **not a power,
but a wound.** Each half feels the absence of the other like a missing limb.

To leave the dark they must ascend together — and only their opposite pulls
(trading surfaces, catching, becoming each other's ground) can carry them up the
very cave that broke them.

---

## Why two · why mirrored · why escape  *(story = mechanics)*

- **Two players** — two halves of one self. Alone, incomplete; together, whole.
- **Mirrored gravity** — the split bound each half to an opposite pole. The flip
  is the wound that severed them, and the only way they move.
- **Escape the cave** — to reach the surface light is to become one again. The
  dark keeps things apart; the light makes them whole.

Every mechanic is the story. Each catch and bridge is the halves reaching for
each other.

---

## The Shadows  *(the threat, explained)*

Not monsters — **other halves that lost their other.** Things split here before,
that never reunited; hollowed out, dissolved into the dark until only a hungry
silhouette remained. They cling to single surfaces, drawn to anything still whole.

To touch one is to be **unmade** — your half lost to the dark (death → the run
begins again). They are a constant warning: *this is what you become if you are
ever truly alone.*

---

## The World — a climb out of the deep

The journey rises toward a far, thin crack of light. The deepest passages are the
most broken — gravity raw, geometry wrong. As the pair ascends:
- worn carvings and still, dead machines hint the cave was once *tended*;
- now and then, frozen in the rock: the silhouettes of **two shapes reaching for
  each other**, never reunited — pairs who failed;
- the light grows, slowly, the higher they climb. **Hope as a gradient.**

---

## The Ending — the Light  *(escape payoff)*

At the cave mouth the two halves step into the light at the same moment and
**fold back into one** — a single small being that walks out under an ordinary
sky, no longer falling either way. The wound is closed.

> *Eerie alt (Limbo/Inside ambiguity):* in the light, only **one** shadow is cast
> where two stood — leaving the player to wonder whether they merged, or whether
> there was only ever one. _(decide later)_

---

## How it's told — wordless

- **Opening vignette:** one figure descends, reaches the place where down turns to
  up, and flashes apart into two that fall opposite ways. The whole premise, no words.
- **Mechanics carry meaning:** catches and bridges *are* the halves reaching.
- **Shadows mirror the pair** in silhouette — same shape, hollowed out.
- **Environmental echoes:** the frozen failed pairs; the brightening light.
- **Ending image** closes the loop — understood, never explained.

---

## Themes

- You cannot leave the dark alone.
- A self has parts that must be carried by each other.
- Wholeness is the reward; separation is the death.

